# Hono Guidelines

Core development guidelines for building type-safe, maintainable APIs using the Hono framework. These patterns ensure consistent, secure, and performant backend services while maintaining strict type safety and clean architecture.

## Core Principles
- **KISS (Keep It Simple, Stupid)** - Always choose the simplest, most maintainable solution
- **TypeScript First** - Always use TypeScript with strict typing (`strict: true`) everywhere
- **Less is More** - Always avoid unnecessary complexity, the best code is no code
- **Functional Programming** - Always use functions over classes, except when classes make clear sense
- **Error Handling** - Always use consistent, typed error handling with clear messages

## Route Organization
- Always structure routes by version and resource using dot notation folders
- Always separate concerns clearly within each route folder
- Always follow consistent naming patterns
- Always maintain clear directory structure
- Always keep route folders flat for better discoverability

✅ Good:
```typescript
// Clear separation of concerns with flat route structure
src/
  app/
    routes/
      v1.users/              # All /v1/users routes
        index.ts            # Route implementations
        schema.ts          # Schema definitions
        lib/               # Business logic
          operations.ts
          validation.ts
          types.ts
      v1.products/          # All /v1/products routes
        index.ts
        schema.ts
        lib/
          operations.ts
          validation.ts
          types.ts
```

❌ Bad:
```typescript
// Wrong: Nested directory structure makes routes hard to find
src/
  routes/              
    v1/                # Wrong: Nested version directory
      users/           # Wrong: Nested resource directory
        index.ts
        [id]/          # Wrong: Deeply nested dynamic routes
          index.ts
          products.ts
    v2/
      products/
        index.ts
```

## Schema Definition
- Always use Zod for request/response schemas
- Always define explicit types for all operations
- Always document API contracts
- Always validate all inputs

✅ Good:
```typescript
// src/app/routes/v1/users/schema.ts
import { z } from 'zod';
import { createRoute } from '@/lib/api';
import { ErrorResponse, SuccessResponse } from '@/lib/responses';

export const SCreateUser = z.object({
  email: z.string().email(),
  name: z.string().min(1)
});

export const CreateUserRoute = createRoute({
  method: 'post',
  path: '/v1/users',
  tags: ['users'],
  summary: 'Create new user',
  request: {
    body: {
      content: {
        'application/json': {
          schema: SCreateUser
        }
      }
    }
  },
  responses: {
    200: SuccessResponse('User created'),
    400: ErrorResponse('Invalid input'),
    401: ErrorResponse('Unauthorized')
  }
});
```

❌ Bad:
```typescript
// Wrong: Missing validation and documentation
router.post('/users', async (c) => {
  const body = await c.req.json();  // Wrong: No schema validation
  return c.json(await db.users.create({ data: body }));  // Wrong: Untyped data
});
```

## Route Implementation
- Always keep all resource routes in a single index.ts file
- Always use descriptive dot notation for route folders
- Always group related route operations in the same folder

✅ Good:
```typescript
// src/app/routes/v1.users/index.ts
import { router } from '@/lib/router';
import { 
  CreateUserRoute, 
  GetUserRoute,
  GetUserProductsRoute,
  CreateUserProductRoute 
} from './schema';
import { 
  createUser, 
  getUser,
  getUserProducts,
  createUserProduct 
} from './lib/operations';

// Base user routes
router.openapi(CreateUserRoute, async (c) => {
  const input = c.req.valid('json');
  return c.json(await createUser(input));
});

router.openapi(GetUserRoute, async (c) => {
  const userId = c.req.param('id');
  return c.json(await getUser(userId));
});

// Nested product routes
router.openapi(GetUserProductsRoute, async (c) => {
  const userId = c.req.param('id');
  return c.json(await getUserProducts(userId));
});

router.openapi(CreateUserProductRoute, async (c) => {
  const userId = c.req.param('id');
  const input = c.req.valid('json');
  return c.json(await createUserProduct(userId, input));
});

// src/app/routes/v1.users/schema.ts
import { z } from 'zod';
import { createRoute } from '@/lib/api';

export const CreateUserRoute = createRoute({
  method: 'post',
  path: '/v1/users',
  // ... schema definition
});

export const GetUserProductsRoute = createRoute({
  method: 'get',
  path: '/v1/users/:id/products',
  // ... schema definition
});

// src/app/routes/v1.users/lib/operations.ts
import { TCreateUserInput, TUser, TProduct } from './types';

export async function createUser(
  input: TCreateUserInput
): Promise<TUser> {
  // Implementation
}

export async function getUserProducts(
  userId: string
): Promise<TProduct[]> {
  // Implementation
}
```

❌ Bad:
```typescript
// Wrong: Routes scattered across multiple files and directories
// src/app/routes/v1/users/index.ts
router.get('/v1/users', async (c) => {
  // Wrong: Route handler in nested directory
});

// Wrong: Schema separated from route
// src/app/schemas/users.ts
export const userSchema = z.object({
  // Wrong: Schema far from usage
});

// Wrong: Operations mixed with routes
// src/app/routes/v1/users/products.ts
export async function getUserProducts() {
  // Wrong: Business logic mixed with route
}
```

## Business Logic
- Always separate business logic from route handlers
- Always use proper types for inputs and outputs
- Always implement proper error handling
- Always validate business rules

✅ Good:
```typescript
// src/app/routes/v1/users/lib/operations.ts
import { TCreateUserInput, TUser } from './types';
import { AppError } from '@/lib/errors';
import { db } from '@/lib/db';

export async function createUser(
  input: TCreateUserInput
): Promise<TUser> {
  // Check for existing user
  const existing = await findUserByEmail(input.email);
  if (existing != null) {
    throw new AppError('#ERR_CONFLICT', 409, {
      description: 'User already exists'
    });
  }
  
  // Create user with validated input
  return db.user.create({
    data: input
  });
}
```

❌ Bad:
```typescript
// Wrong: Missing types and improper error handling
export async function createUser(input: any) {  // Wrong: Untyped input
  if (!input.email) {
    throw new Error('Invalid email');  // Wrong: Generic error
  }
  return db.user.create({ data: input });  // Wrong: Unvalidated data
}
```

## Error Handling
- Always use the centralized error handling system
- Always throw typed errors from the `@repo/hono-utils` module
- Always include error codes for client-side handling
- Never expose internal error details to clients

✅ Good:
```typescript
// src/app/routes/v1.users/lib/operations.ts
import { AppError } from '@repo/hono-utils';

export async function createUser(input: TCreateUserInput): Promise<TUser> {
  const existing = await db.user.findUnique({ 
    where: { email: input.email } 
  });
  
  if (existing) {
    throw new AppError('#ERR_USER_EXISTS', 409, {
      message: 'A user with this email already exists'
    });
  }
  
  try {
    return await db.user.create({ data: input });
  } catch (error) {
    throw new AppError('#ERR_USER_CREATE_FAILED', 500);
  }
}

// src/app/routes/v1.users/index.ts
router.openapi(CreateUserRoute, async (c) => {
  try {
    const input = c.req.valid('json');
    const user = await createUser(input);
    return c.json(user);
  } catch (error) {
    // Error handler middleware will catch and format the error
    throw error;
  }
});
```

❌ Bad:
```typescript
// Wrong: Inconsistent error handling
router.post('/users', async (c) => {
  try {
    const data = await c.req.json();
    const user = await db.user.create({ data });
    return c.json(user);
  } catch (e) {
    // Wrong: Exposing internal error details
    return c.json({ error: e.message }, 500);
  }
});
```

## Input Validation
- Always define request schemas using Zod
- Always validate request body, query, and params
- Always use typed schemas with OpenAPI definitions
- Never trust client input without validation

✅ Good:
```typescript
// src/app/routes/v1.users/schema.ts
import { z } from 'zod';
import { createRoute } from '@/lib/api';

export const SCreateUserInput = z.object({
  email: z.string().email(),
  name: z.string().min(2),
  role: z.enum(['USER', 'ADMIN'])
});

export const CreateUserRoute = createRoute({
  method: 'post',
  path: '/v1/users',
  request: {
    body: SCreateUserInput
  },
  responses: {
    201: z.object({
      user: SUser
    })
  }
});
```

❌ Bad:
```typescript
// Wrong: No schema validation
router.post('/users', async (c) => {
  const data = await c.req.json();
  // Wrong: Using unvalidated input directly
  return await db.user.create({ data });
});
```

## Authentication & Authorization
- Always validate session in middleware
- Always check permissions before operations
- Always use role-based access control
- Never expose sensitive operations without auth

✅ Good:
```typescript
// src/app/routes/v1.users/index.ts
import { getClientSession } from '@/lib';

router.openapi(CreateUserRoute, async (c) => {
    const session = await getClientSession(c);
  if (!session.hasSession()) {
    throw new AppError('#ERR_UNAUTHORIZED', 401);
  }

  const input = c.req.valid('json');
  return c.json(await createUser(input));
});
```

❌ Bad:
```typescript
// Wrong: No authentication or authorization
router.post('/admin/users', async (c) => {
  // Wrong: Sensitive operation without auth
  const users = await db.user.findMany();
  return c.json(users);
});
```

